bin/
dist/
cover.out
# `go build ./some/package` with no -o writes the binary into the working
# directory, named after the package. Two test fixtures are real programs
# — the bridge's echo extension and the renderer's stand-in gpg — and
# building one to check it compiles leaves a multi-megabyte binary at the
# repository root. Both were committed once.
*.exe
/halite-hub
/halite-node
/halite-api

# migrate reports: they quote a real tree's file paths and service names,
# which belong to whoever owns that estate and not in this repository.
complex-tree.txt

# OpenTofu and Terraform variable files, anywhere in the tree.
#
# A .tfvars is where a credential ends up, whatever the surrounding
# README says: it is the file the tooling reads by default, and
# `terraform.tfvars` and `*.auto.tfvars` are both loaded with no flag to
# say so. contrib/tofu takes its token from VULTR_API_KEY in the
# environment and needs no variables file at all, so one appearing
# anywhere is either a mistake or a secret, and neither is worth the
# chance that it is committed from a directory nobody thought to cover.
#
# Tree-wide rather than per-directory on purpose. This lived only in
# contrib/tofu/.gitignore, which left `terraform.tfvars` at the
# repository root, or under any infra directory added later, perfectly
# committable.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json

# And `.env`, for the same reason and more so: it is the filename
# `git add -A` sweeps up, and nothing in this repository reads one. The
# lab reads its token from the environment, or from a file under $HOME
# that no commit here can reach -- see LAB_ENV in the Makefile.
.env
.env.*
*.env
